        "summary":  "The article argues that AI will strengthen rather than displace SaaS platforms, citing how companies like Shopify use AI-powered customization tools to let customers build extensions, which increases platform stickiness. The author contends that while AI makes coding cheaper and more accessible, the real value in SaaS lies in established platforms that provide stability, support, and infrastructure. SaaS companies that allow customers to use AI for customization see increased interest in the core product, not abandonment of it.",
        "execSummary":  "For enterprise technology leaders evaluating whether AI-native competitors will undercut their SaaS vendor base, this article offers a counterintuitive but well-argued position: AI lowers the cost of customization but raises the value of the platform that hosts it. The Shopify/Sidekick example is directly applicable to enterprise software — CRM, ITSM, and support platforms that expose extensible APIs and AI customization tools are likely to become more entrenched, not less. The strategic implication is that enterprise SaaS consolidation around platforms with strong AI extensibility is the more likely outcome than wholesale replacement. Caveat: the argument assumes incumbents move fast enough on AI integration to outpace AI-native entrants.",
