        "summary":  "Sovereign AI refers to organizational control over AI development, deployment, and governance through in-house resources and infrastructure rather than dependence on external vendors. The concept addresses concerns around cost management, data privacy, and vendor lock-in, though only 13% of surveyed organizations report widespread understanding of the term. The article outlines why enterprises and governments are increasingly pursuing sovereign AI postures as geopolitical and regulatory pressures intensify.",
        "execSummary":  "Sovereign AI is moving from a government-level concern to a mainstream enterprise strategy question — the fact that only 13% of organizations broadly understand the concept signals both the urgency of the education gap and the competitive advantage available to leaders who act on it now. For senior enterprise leaders, sovereign AI is fundamentally a risk management question: how much control over your AI infrastructure, data, and governance do you need to meet regulatory, competitive, and operational requirements? The article is a timely prompt to audit current vendor dependencies in your AI stack against your organization\u0027s tolerance for lock-in, data exposure, and cost volatility. The practical implication is that enterprises should be developing explicit AI infrastructure sovereignty policies before regulatory mandates force reactive ones.",
